                <pb facs="0325" n="253" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg253"/>

                <head>No. 28.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Earl Granville to Mr. Lowell.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Foreign Office</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-03-07"><hi rendition="#italic">March</hi> 7,
                        1882</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to acknowledge the receipt
                    of your letter of the 24th ultimo, forwarding a copy of a resolution of the
                    United States House of Representatives, calling for information concerning the
                    arrest and imprisonment of American citizens by the British Government.</p>
                <p>In reply, I beg leave to acquaint you that this matter has been referred to the
                    proper department of Her Majesty’s Government.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>GRANVILLE.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
